              ":question/id": "85913448-bf44-5c48-a845-edf7c36b2c7d",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-4902",
              ":question/problem": "Write $5^{2/3}$ using radical notation.",
              ":question/worked-solution": "First, using the power law for exponents, we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n5^{2/3} = 5^{2\\cdot (1/3)} = \\left(5^2\\right)^{1/3}.\n$$\n\nThe fractional exponent $x^{1/n}$ can be written as the radical $\\sqrt[n]{x}.$\n\nComparing $x^{1/n}$ with our expression above, we get $x = 5^2$ and $n = 3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\left(5^2\\right)^{1/3} = \\sqrt[3]{5^2}.\n$$"
            },
            ":knowledge-point/id": "285a1b25-5f08-562c-8dc8-820d7de1f963",
            ":knowledge-point/title": "Converting a Fractional Exponent to a Radical"
          },
          {
